  "query_preview": "..\n\nBroad area of surface high pressure continues to build south from \nOntario late this afternoon and will maintain influence throughout \nthe remainder of the weekend. Lingering axis of higher low level \nmoisture quality still extends from near Grand Rapids southeast into \nthe I-94 to Ohio border corridor late this afternoon. There remains \npotential for a few isolated showers to emerge yet through peak \nheating along this gradient through early evening. Otherwise, deep \nlayer stability and subsidence will dominate through this period. \nLingering late day diurnal cu will fade into the evening with the \nloss of daytime heating and steady influx of drier air. Low \ntemperatures overnight will range from the mid-50s across northern \nand rural areas to the lower 60s closer to the Detroit metro area. \nHigh pressure anchors directly over the central Great Lakes on \nSunday. Mostly sunny skies during the day will yield seasonable \nhighs in the upper 70s with dew points remaining comfortably in the \n50s. \n\nLow-to-mid level ridging over the central Plains will drift eastward \ndirectly over the Great Lakes early in the week. Broad subsidence \nand a very dry atmospheric column will maintain abundant sunshine \nand pleasant conditions across southeast Michigan on Monday. High \ntemperatures will remain at near seasonal norms in the upper 70s \nwith dew points remaining capped in the upper 50s to lower 60s. By \nTuesday, the high pressure center shifts east toward the Atlantic \nseaboard, allowing low-level flow to veer around to the south-\nsouthwest. Warm air advection across the Ohio Valley will lift \ndaytime highs back into the lower 80s. The daytime hours on Tuesday \nare expected to remain mostly dry under warm return flow, though \nhigh clouds will gradually thicken from west to east by late \nafternoon. \n\nA strong Pacific upper-level disturbance will traverse southern \nCanada, flattening the Great Lakes ridge and driving a surface low \nacross Ontario. An accompanying cold front will sweep south through \nlower Michigan Wednesday. Moisture return ahead of the boundary will \npush dew points back up, supporting the next round of scattered \nshowers and rumbles of thunder starting late Tuesday night and \npeaking on Wednesday. Temperatures will spike into the mid-to-upper \n80s Wednesday ahead of the frontal passage before cooler, drier air \nreturns for the second half of the week.",
